Now we can use 'stack 14' to see if our gadget's constraints will be met.

Stack pointer plus 0x50 isn't null,

but we know it's still safe since it's only argv[0]

and the address is readable.

Unfortunately argv[1] isn't null either,

which was how we got away with using this gadget last time.

But it does point to memory that we control.

Notice how the next three argv arrays are the same values I've been using to mark chunks, with these

capital L's corresponding to argv[1].

This situation is, of course, program dependent,

you might not be so lucky with different binaries.

In this case, we're fortunate because it means we can manipulate the first argument passed to the dash

shell and potentially avoid it failing.

If you've been experimenting with dash, you'll have noticed that it's sensitive to malformed arguments.

Passing a nonexistent option or file name to it will not result in a shell.

What's worse is that even if the first argument is valid, it will continue to parse arguments until

it hits a null, failing on any that are malformed or unreadable.

If you've been browsing the dash man page, however, you may have discovered a couple of solutions

to this problem.

The first is that if dash comes across a valid file name, it will attempt to parse it for further

commands, ignoring any succeeding arguments.

So if you're able to write your commands to disk, then this is a viable option.

A second, perhaps more practical solution is to pass the

'-s' argument which starts reading commands from stdin, ignoring any succeeding arguments.

So I'll return to my editor and swap in the line that writes the address of our one-gadget into the

malloc hook.

Running the script now

prints the error "M can't open L". Those strings come from the data I used to mark chunks in my script,

so if I replace the "L" marker with the '-s' option, not forgetting to null-terminate the string,

then run the script under production conditions without the GDB and NOASLR options,

I finally get a shell and hopefully so do you.

To summarize, our binary had a double-free bug and a libc leak, but wouldn't allow us to request

0x70-sized chunks.

So we leveraged the double-free to make two fastbin dup attacks.

We used the first to write a fake size field into the main arena,

then the second to allocate into the main arena, overwriting the top chunk pointer.

We aligned the top chunk pointer with a reliable libc address so that our fake top chunk had a size

field that pased the top chunk size

integrity check.

Then we made a request from that top chunk, overwriting the malloc hook with the address of a one-gadget.

The one-gadget constraints couldn't be met, but because we controlled the first argument passed to

the dash shell that is invoked, we were able to pass the stdin option, avoiding dash parsing

any malformed arguments and finally giving us our shell.

A working exploit for this challenge, complete with comments, is available from this section's resources.

Don't forget to review the PDF

if you need to refresh your memory on arena layouts, the fastbins, the fastbin dup technique

or any of the pwndbg commands and pwntools functions we've covered so far.

There is one big advantage to controlling two or more arguments passed to a one-gadget. Say we're attacking

a setuid binary, I'll turn the challenge binary into one now.

If I run my exploit, the shell I get still belongs to the user

I run the script as, that's because most modern shells will reset your effective UID

if it doesn't match your actual UID.

This is a safety feature to protect against exactly this sort of situation. When using the system() function

there isn't much we can do about this since by the time we've invoked a shell

the initial call to execve("/bin/sh") will already have reset our effective UID, but when using a

one-gadget, we're passing arguments straight to that initial invocation of dash.

This means we can pass the '-p' option, which disables this behavior.

I'll add the 'priv' option to my script in

what will become argv[1], keeping the stdin option which we still need, at what will become

argv[2].

Now when I run the script

I get a shell.

But this time it doesn't reset my effective UID.

That last part isn't necessary to solve the challenge, but it's a nice trick you can do with one-gadgets.

